  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <note id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00001">Access the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b62c9150250bf52d6cc37569213ab516e0090dca">Desmos guide PDF</link> for tips on solving problems with the Desmos graphing calculator.</para>
      </note>
      <para id="para-00002">In an exponential function, the output is multiplied by the same factor every time the input increases by one. The multiplier is called the growth factor.</para>
      <para id="para-00003">Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Refer back to your work in the table in activity 5.4.3: Exponential Change: The Growth Factor, where \(n=500 \cdot 2^t\) and \(p=100 \cdot 3^t\). Use that information and the given coordinate planes to graph the following using the Desmos tool below.</para>
      <para id="para-00004">Use the equation \(n=500 \cdot 2^t\) to answer questions 1 – 7</para><media alt="insert alt text">
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/febe5da3890f275ffd7a99061e734d10f5842e5b.html" width="500" height="400"/></media><!--Q#1-->
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00005">What is the domain?</para>
            <para id="para-00006">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00008">The graph of the function shows that all real values of t define the domain. \((-\infty, +\infty)\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00009">What is the range?</para>
            <para id="para-00010">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00012">The graph of the function shows that all real values of n greater than 0 define the range. \((0, +\infty)\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00013">The scenario dictates that the domain and range be restricted in comparison to the graph of the function that models it. Write a reasonable domain and range based on the scenario using inequalities.</para>
            <para id="para-00014">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00016">Domain: \(0 \leq t &lt; +\infty\); because we begin measuring time at 0 hours.</para>
            <para id="para-00017">Range: \(500 \leq n \lt +\infty\); because we begin with 500 bacteria in the lab experiment.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>Graph \((t,n)\) when \(t\) is 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4.</item>
      </list>
<media alt="insert alt text">      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/febe5da3890f275ffd7a99061e734d10f5842e5b.html" width="500" height="400"/></media>
